@import "./compiler.sly"
@use Build

class ProcessUtils {
    Runs a command, given as the words of its command line, with an optional working directory, an
    optional text handed over stdin and an optional timeout, and returns its exit code, its stdout and
    its stderr once it exits. A command that cannot be spawned returns exit code 127 and the reason as
    its stderr.

    Every command runs in a process group of its own, killed whole once the command exits and once it
    outruns its timeout, so nothing it spawned outlives it: a script that runs another, which runs it
    back, dies with the command instead of multiplying behind it. A command that outruns its timeout
    returns the exit code that says so, and one given no timeout is waited on for as long as it runs.
}

class SlytherRuntime {
    How a deterministic step of a given lang is run, checked and installed. It is created with the lang and
    the folder the artifacts are built into, and throws when the lang is not one it knows. The langs are:

    - ts: run with `bun`, checked with `bun build --no-bundle`, dependencies in a package.json installed with
      `bun install`, ignoring node_modules
    - py: run with the python of the `.venv` of the artifacts folder, checked with `py_compile`, dependencies in
      a requirements.txt installed with `pip` into the `.venv`, which is created when missing, ignoring `.venv`
      and the `__pycache__` folders compiling leaves next to the scripts
    - sh: run with `sh`, checked with `sh -n`, no dependencies

    Every command it returns has its paths relative to the root of the project, since that is where every
    step runs: a step prints paths relative to the root, so its working directory is the root.

    It gives the extension of a script of the lang, the command that runs a script, the command that checks
    its syntax, the file that declares the dependencies of every step of the lang, as a name and a content
    built from a `name: version` map, and the command that installs them, if the lang has any.
}

class SlytherGenerator {
    What writes the scripts and the markdown a step needs, as an abstract class every backend implements
    with two primitives: `ask`, which sends a prompt and returns a JSON reply that matches a schema, along
    with a session that a later `ask` may resume so a fix continues the same conversation, and `execute`,
    which runs a prompt with the tools to read and edit the project, for the llm steps of an operation,
    and returns the text of the reply along with a session that a later `execute` may resume, for the
    same reason.

    On top of them it defines `generate`, which takes a #{SlytherGenerationTask} and returns the files it
    asked for, the dependencies they declare and the session, and `fix`, which resumes the session with the
    reason the files failed and returns them corrected. Both throw when a reply lacks an expected file or
    adds one that was not asked for.

    It also defines `review`, which asks, in a session of its own so the writer never approves itself,
    whether the files do what the instructions say, reading them without running them, and returns a
    verdict of pass and the errors found.

    Every call names the role it is made for and who plays it, a cast of #{Compiler::SlytherRole}, which is
    who is asked. `problemsWith` says, before any work is asked for, what keeps the generator from asking
    for the work of the given casts, one line per problem, and says nothing unless a generator knows better.
    `pin` returns the options a role is played with, with whatever would resolve differently from one run
    to the next, like a model named by an alias, pinned to what it resolves to now, and `pinRoles` pins
    who plays each of the roles named, asking for each set of options once; both leave everything as it
    is unless a generator knows better. #{SlytherProviders} pins each role with its own provider.
}

class SlytherProviders {
    The #{SlytherGenerator} a project runs with: it hands every call to the provider that plays the role of
    its cast, making each provider once. The providers are claude-cli, a #{ClaudeCLIGenerator}. Its
    `problemsWith` names, once per role, a role nobody plays, with the `@role` line that would bind it, a
    provider that is not one of these, and whatever the provider says of the options it is handed. A call
    whose role nobody plays, or with no role, must throw an error, since no model is ever picked for it.
}

class SlytherTracedGenerator {
    A #{SlytherGenerator} that wraps another and hands it every call, printing, through the log it is
    given, each prompt before it is sent, with the session it resumes if any, and each reply after, with
    how long it took and the session it opened: the JSON of an `ask`, the text of an `execute`. It is
    what --verbose turns on.
}

class SlytherGenerationTask {
    What a #{SlytherGenerator} is asked to write, contains:

    - instructions: The prose of what to write: the rules of the kind, the operation, the step, its params
      and its lang, and the conventions every script follows
    - context: The files the generator may need to read, each with its path and its content, gathered by
      Slyther beforehand so the build never depends on what a backend chooses to explore
    - expected: The paths of the files the reply must contain, relative to the root of the project
    - dependencies: The `name: version` map of what is already installed for the lang, so the reply
      reuses it instead of adding another version or another package that does the same
}

class ClaudeCLIGenerator {
    A #{SlytherGenerator} backed by the Claude Code CLI, `claude` unless the SLYTHER_CLAUDE environment
    variable names another executable, asking the model and with the effort the options of whoever plays
    the role of the call name, and never the default of the CLI, which is whatever whoever runs it set. It
    takes the options model, which it requires, and effort; any other is a problem, and so is a model
    named by an alias the CLI resolves by whoever runs it, default, best or opusplan, since two people
    would ask different models. The alias of a family, opus, sonnet, haiku or fable, with or without the
    context window it asks for, is taken: `pin` asks it the smallest prompt there is and reads, among the
    models the reply says it used, the one of its family, since the CLI also asks a small model of its
    own, keeping the context window the alias asked for. None or more than one of the family must throw
    an error rather than guess. A full id is pinned as it is, with no call. `ask` runs it in print mode with every tool disabled, the prompt
    on stdin, the JSON output format and the schema as its structured output, resuming the session when
    given one, and returns the structured output and the session id of the reply. `execute` runs it in
    print mode in the given directory with the tools to read, search and edit files and to run commands
    allowed, resuming the session when given one, and returns the text and the session id of the reply.
    Either throws when the CLI does not reply, or replies with an error.
}

class SlytherVerifier {
    Checks that the script written for a deterministic step works before it is recorded as built, and
    returns why it does not, or nothing when it does. It is created with the root of the project.

    Every script is checked for syntax with the command of its #{SlytherRuntime}, and every run it makes
    is given a timeout, after which the script is killed, with everything it spawned, and fails, so a
    script that hangs or that spawns without end never passes. The scripts of the
    read-only operations, locate, list, signature and uses, are also run, since running them changes
    nothing: list with no args must exit 0, and, when the id alone locates an artifact of the kind, locate
    must find the first id list prints, so list prints the ids locate takes; the others must exit 0 or 1
    given the first id list prints, or a sample id when it prints none, with a sample value for every
    param they take after the id, and print nothing when they exit 1; signature and uses must exit 0 given
    an id list printed, since that artifact exists.

    A sample says nothing of a script that reads the wrong arg, or asks for one it is never given, since
    either exits 1 as a missing artifact does. So when the id list prints is an instance the project
    declares, locate, signature and uses are run with the args that instance runs with, filled as the
    check fills them, and must exit 0, since list says it exists; and list runs locate with them too,
    whether or not locate takes the id alone. An id no instance declares keeps its samples. When
    signature exits 0, every line it prints must
    have the `key shape` form, and when uses exits 0, every line must have the `kind:id key` form.

    The expand of a composite kind is also run, with a sample value for each of its params: it must exit 0
    and what it prints must be read by #{Compiler::SlytherExpansion} without error, for an instance named
    after the sample.
}

class SlytherArtifactManifest {
    What a build remembers, written as manifest.json in the artifacts folder, contains:

    - operations: Every operation built, keyed by `kind::name`, with its kind, its name, whether it is
      deterministic, its params, the markdown that orchestrates it when it is not deterministic, and its
      steps in order, each with its name, its kind, its path relative to the artifacts folder, for a
      deterministic one, its lang and the command that runs it, and, for the check of a rule, the name
      of the rule, so whoever runs it knows to hand it the segments of the instance rather than the params
    - scripts: Every #{Compiler::SlytherRunScript} built, keyed by its name, with its name, its params and its
      steps in order, each with its name, its path relative to the artifacts folder, its lang and the
      command that runs it
    - files: Every file built, keyed by its path relative to the artifacts folder, with the input hash it
      was built from, the output hash of the content it was written with, and the dependencies it declares

    Loading a manifest that does not exist gives an empty one. It is saved with its entries sorted, so it
    diffs well, and gives the union of the dependencies declared by every file of a lang, one version per
    package.
}

class SlytherArtifactBuilder {
    Brings the artifacts folder in line with a list of #{Compiler::SlytherArtifactKind}, given the root of
    the project, the folder relative to it and a #{SlytherGenerator}. Every kind with operations gets a
    folder per operation, `kind/operation`, where the kind is its name with the `::` of its namespace
    written as a dot, holding a script per deterministic step, named after the step
    with the extension of its #{SlytherRuntime}, a markdown per llm step, and operation.md for an
    operation that is not deterministic. The operations locate, list, signature and uses are built before
    the others, since the others may run them.

    A script waits only for the scripts of the operations it is offered, since it may run them and its
    verification runs list and locate, so everything else is written side by side, up to four scripts at
    once unless the build is given another number: the kinds never wait for each other, and within a kind
    locate is built first, then list, then signature and uses at once, since neither needs the other, and
    then every other operation at once. The scripts that run the project wait for everything before them.
    Installs and verifications run one at a time, since an install changes what every script runs with,
    and the manifest is saved after every file, one save after the other. Once a script fails, nothing
    that has not started is written, what is under way finishes and is recorded, and the first failure
    is what the build fails with.

    The input hash of a step is the hash of its closure hash, the scope hash of its operation, what it
    is shown of its kind, its lang and its path, so it changes when the step, the operation or what it
    is shown change and not when a sibling step does. A deterministic step is shown the guidance of its
    kind and the rules it references, so its hash holds the guidance hash of the kind and its own
    closure hash covers the rules; one that references no rule is shown every rule, so its hash holds
    the rules hash of the kind instead, as the hash of every llm step does, since a writer is shown
    everything. The scripts of the operations that read an instance, locate, list, signature, uses and
    expand, are shown the guidance alone and hash with it, whatever they reference: what they find is
    not what must be true of it, so a rule that changes never rewrites them. The input hash of operation.md is the hash of the closure hash of the operation, the
    rules hash of the kind, its steps and its path, so it is written again when a rule is added.

    The check of a rule, a step of evaluate that names it, is built from the rule alone: the input hash
    of its script is the hash of the closure hash of the rule, of every negative that references it, of
    the scope hash of whoever owns the rule, its lang and its path, so editing a rule rebuilds its check
    and nothing else, and adopting a rule from a trait builds its check once per kind that adopts it,
    under that kind. Its script takes no params: it is run with every segment locate printed for the
    instance, as locate printed them, and judges only those lines, with the file around a range as
    context and never as what is judged, and finds the args of the instance in its environment, one
    variable per param of evaluate named `SLYTHER_PARAM`, which may all be absent, as when it is run
    against a negative: it then judges what the segments alone say. Its instructions hold the rule, the prose of the trait or the
    kind that owns it, the negatives that reference it as what it must reject, the lang and the
    conventions; once it passes its verification, it is run against every negative that references its
    rule, written to a file of its own with the segment naming that file, and must exit with a code
    other than 0 for each, otherwise the build sends it back to be fixed as a script that fails
    verification is, saying which negative it accepted. The file holds the fenced code of the
    negative, and is named as the fence names it after its lang, as in ```` ```ts index.ts ````, since a
    rule that speaks of a file by name must find the negative under that name; a fence that names
    none gives the file the extension of its lang. A rule speaks of some of the segments of an
    artifact, which the check finds by their path or name and judges, passing over the rest. The markdown of an llm rule holds the rule, the
    prose of its owner, its negatives as what breaks it, and says to judge that rule alone.

    A file is built when the #{SlytherArtifactManifest} does not know it, when its input hash changed,
    when it is missing, or when the hash of its content is not the output hash it was written with,
    which means it was edited by hand. A file the manifest knows that no kind asks for anymore is
    removed, along with the folders left empty. Anything else is kept, and the build reports what it
    did to every file.

    The markdown of an llm step and operation.md are written by Slyther itself: the first holds the rules
    of the kind, the params of the operation and the prose of the step, the second lists the steps in
    order, as the command to run for a deterministic one, with every param as `"{name}"`, and as the
    markdown to follow for an llm one. The rules of the kind, wherever a writer is shown them, are its
    guidance followed by every rule, each under its name and saying whether a script or a judge checks
    it, the prose of every trait adopted before the rules it brings, and every negative as what never to
    write. A script is asked from the generator with a
    #{SlytherGenerationTask}: its instructions hold the guidance of the kind and the rules the step or
    its operation reference, or every rule when they reference none, the operation and its params,
    the step, the conventions every script follows and the commands that run the deterministic operations
    of the kind built before it, save that signature and uses are never offered each other, and never the
    ones built after, so what a script may run is what was written
    without knowing of it and no script can run one that runs it back, and its context holds the prose of
    every artifact the kind, the operation or the step reference. The generator reads no files while it writes a script, so wherever
    these hold the prose of a `ref`, they hold the text of its file instead, while the markdown of an llm
    step and operation.md, which are followed with tools, only point at it. Once written, the dependencies the reply declares are recorded, the
    dependencies file of the lang is rewritten when their union changed and installed, and the script is
    checked with #{SlytherVerifier}: when it fails, the failure is sent back with `fix`, up to three
    attempts, after which the build throws and the file is not recorded, so the next build tries again.

    The expand of a composite kind is built as any deterministic operation, and its instructions also
    hold, for every kind its prose references, the rules of the kind and the params of its create, since
    what it prints must declare what create needs, and the form of a declaration as
    #{Compiler::SlytherExpansion} reads it.

    Whatever is built for an operation of a demanded kind that takes the demands, a script or a markdown,
    is also told what that param holds: nothing declares an instance of such a kind, so its demands are
    the whole of what the instance must do, and what they hold is said outright rather than left to the
    rules of the kind to mention.

    Every #{Compiler::SlytherRunScript} it is given gets a folder `@run/name`, which no kind can take, holding
    a script per step, built after every operation so it may run them. The input hash of a step of a
    script is the hash of its closure hash, the scope hash of its script, its lang and its path. It is
    asked from the generator with the prose of the script and of the step, its params, the conventions of a
    script that runs the project, which may run for as long as the project does, and the commands of every
    deterministic operation built, and its context holds the prose of every artifact the script references.
    It is checked for syntax, never run, since it may never exit, and then reviewed with the `review` of the
    generator against those instructions: its errors are sent back with `fix` as any other failure.

    The manifest is saved after every file is built, so a build that fails halfway keeps what it did, and
    a .gitignore inside the artifacts folder ignores whatever the runtimes in use create.
}
